  "verbalization": "Tolbutamide, a sulfonylurea compound, is linked to Abcc8 through a shared pathway involving antidiabetic agents. The evidence suggests that Tolbutamide, Glipizide, and Glyburide, all part of the sulfonylurea class, bind to Abcc8. This binding is supported by multiple paths, with the lowest common ancestor of Tolbutamide and its metabolites being the N-Sulfonylurea class. The consistent presence of Abcc8 as a target in these pathways indicates a potential mechanism by which sulfonylurea drugs interact with cellular transporters, possibly through a common molecular interaction site. The relationship between Tolbutamide and Abcc8 is further reinforced by the causal link in Path 3, where Tolbutamide causes Porphyria Hepatic, which in turn is associated with Glipizide, both of which bind to Abcc8. This integrated evidence suggests a plausible mechanism involving sulfonylurea compounds interacting with Abcc8 through a shared biological pathway, highlighting the role of Abcc8 in mediating drug effects.",
